{
  "dom_id": "step-q216789",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Select the correct options in the truth table below and use this to determine whether $[¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)]∧(R∨S)$ is a contradiction.\n\n| $\\,R\\,$ | $\\,S\\,$ | $\\,¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)\\,$ | $\\,R∨S\\,$ | $\\,[¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)]∧(R∨S)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nAccording to the table, $[¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)]∧(R∨S)$ \n{{field-5}}\nalways false. Therefore, it \n{{field-6}} \na contradiction.",
  "worked_solution": "An expression is a contradiction if it is always false, regardless of the inputs.\n\nBy completing the final column in the truth table below, we obtain the following:\n\n| $\\,R\\,$ | $\\,S\\,$ | $\\,¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)\\,$ | $\\,R∨S\\,$ | $\\,[¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)]∧(R∨S)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $[¬(R∧S)∧(¬R⇒¬S)]∧(R∨S)$ \n$\\text{isn't}$\nalways false. Therefore, it \n$\\text{isn't}$\na contradiction.",
